Phase 1 — Configure NGINX; Configure NGINX to receive client requests and forward them to backend application services.

NGINX performs the following functions in this lab:
Listens for incoming HTTP requests.
Acts as the public-facing web entry point.
Forwards requests to backend application instances.
Distributes requests across the available application containers.
Adds forwarding headers containing client and request information.
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________
____________________________________________________________________________________________________________

The NGINX configuration is located at:
nginx/default.conf

The upstream block defines the three backend application instances:

upstream ccgiant_apps {

    server app1:3000 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;

    server app2:3000 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;

    server app3:3000 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;

    keepalive 16;
}

Important terminology:
upstream: Defines a group of backend servers.
server: Identifies an application endpoint.
max_fails: Sets the failure threshold used by NGINX's passive failure tracking.
fail_timeout: Defines the failure tracking and temporary unavailability period.
keepalive: Allows reusable connections to upstream servers.
NGINX uses round-robin distribution by default when no alternative balancing method is specified.
______________________________________________________________________________________________________________
______________________________________________________________________________________________________________ 

Reverse proxy configuration;

server {

    listen 80;

    server_name _;

    location / {

        proxy_pass http://ccgiant_apps;

        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        proxy_set_header Connection "";

        proxy_set_header Host $host;

        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;

        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;

        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        proxy_connect_timeout 5s;

        proxy_read_timeout 30s;

        proxy_next_upstream error timeout http_502 http_503 http_504;

        add_header X-Upstream $upstream_addr always;
    }
}
______________________________________________________________________________________________________________
_______________________________________________________________________________________________________________ 

Important directives;

listen 80------------------------NGINX listens on port 80 inside its container
server_name _  ------------------Default catch-all server name
location /  ---------------------Matches requests to the root URL and its paths
proxy_pass ----------------------Forwards requests to the upstream group
proxy_set_header ----------------Passes request information to backend applications
proxy_connect_timeout -----------Sets the upstream connection timeout
proxy_read_timeout --------------Sets the upstream response-read timeout
proxy_next_upstream -------------Defines conditions under which NGINX can retry another upstream
add_header ----------------------Adds the selected upstream address to the response

Operational note: NGINX passive failure tracking is not equivalent to a complete active health-check system.
Docker Compose separately monitors the application containers through their health checks.

